---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="celsius-to-fahrenheit">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="celsius-to-fahrenheit" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why you cannot just add a number</h2>
    <p>
      Celsius and Fahrenheit differ in two ways: the position of zero and the size of a degree. Celsius
      puts zero at the freezing point of water and 100 at its boiling point, so there are a hundred
      degrees between them. Fahrenheit puts the same two points at 32 and 212, so there are a hundred
      and eighty. A Fahrenheit degree is thus only 5/9 the size of a Celsius degree.
    </p>
    <p>The formula has two parts, in this order:</p>
    <p><strong>&deg;F = (&deg;C &times; 9/5) + 32</strong></p>
    <p>
      Scale first, then shift. If you add 32 before you multiply, the answer is always 25.6 degrees too
      high.
    </p>

    <h2>The anchor points</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">&deg;C</th><th scope="col">&deg;F</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>-40</td><td>-40</td><td>The only point where the scales agree</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>32</td><td>Water freezes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>39.2</td><td>Fridge target temperature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>16</td><td>60.8</td><td>Chilly indoors</td></tr>
        <tr><td>20</td><td>68</td><td>Comfortable room temperature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>37</td><td>98.6</td><td>Normal body temperature</td></tr>
        <tr><td>38</td><td>100.4</td><td>Fever threshold</td></tr>
        <tr><td>100</td><td>212</td><td>Water boils at sea level</td></tr>
        <tr><td>180</td><td>356</td><td>A moderate oven</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Mental conversion that is actually good enough</h2>
    <p>
      Double it and add 30. 20 &rarr; 70 (true 68). 25 &rarr; 80 (true 77). 30 &rarr; 90 (true 86). The
      error grows with temperature because you use 2 instead of 1.8. It is within a few degrees for
      weather and useless for an oven. For cold values it is very good: 5 &rarr; 40 against a true 41.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exact mental method: subtract a tenth of the number, double it, add 32. For 20: 20 minus 2 is
      18, doubled is 36, plus 32 is 68.
    </p>

    <h2>Differences convert differently</h2>
    <p>
      A temperature change has no zero point to shift, so a difference converts with the 9/5 factor
      alone. A rise of 10 &deg;C is a rise of 18 &deg;F, not 50. People often get this wrong in weather
      reports and engineering specifications, where "a 5 degree tolerance" means very different things
      in the two scales.
    </p>

    <h2>Where each scale is still used</h2>
    <p>
      Fahrenheit is the everyday scale in the United States and some of its territories, and is used
      informally in the Cayman Islands, Liberia and parts of the Caribbean. All other countries use
      Celsius, as do US science and medicine. The UK uses both: forecasts are in Celsius, but people
      still describe a hot summer day as "in the nineties".
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
